WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.700
It's hard to think of
any athlete better known

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.259
or more polarizing than Alex Rodriguez.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:09.730
He's done it again.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.600
Swatted the ball out of Arroyo's hand.

00:00:13.651 --> 00:00:17.150
See, he joins a 3000 hit club.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.340
A three time MVP,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.350
14 time all star and
world series champion,

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.160
he retired in 2016 as
the highest paid player

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.630
in MLB history.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.790
Fourth all time on the list of home runs.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.280
But his playing career isn't
likely to be remembered

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.880
by those statistics or by
who he is or isn't dating.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.390
A record suspension
tonight for baseball star,

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.750
Alex Rodriguez, over his use
of performance-enhancing drugs.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.998
You say you did not do these PEDs

00:00:43.998 --> 00:00:45.078
that they're accusing you of doing?

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.560
You're correct, Mike.
Okay.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.219
This dude is unbelievable.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:51.830
A phony to the end.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.570
While it was the most
painful time of my life,

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.300
it may cost me the hall of fame,

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.113
and that's on me, nobody else.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.490
I think in a weird way,
I'm gonna look back and say

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.180
that's also one of the
best things that happened.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.750
Five years after his retirement,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.440
Alex Rodriguez, the CEO, is
getting back into sports,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.770
this time as an owner of
a pro basketball team.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.870
And he wants to build more
than just a franchise,

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.670
but a legacy maybe with
a dose of redemption.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.600
The mistakes made in baseball are done

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.200
and I can't go back and fix 'em.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:30.242
I wanna keep a clean record
as a father, as a friend,

00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:31.890
as a business person.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:32.910
And because of those mistakes,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.846
I think I have that opportunity

00:01:34.846 --> 00:01:36.993
and I'm having the time
of my life doing it.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.260
A-Rod, Alex Rodriguez is buying

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.210
the Minnesota Timberwolves
and the Minnesota Lynx.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.740
The purchase price is expected to be

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.265
in the range of $1.5 billion.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.130
You know, for me, I think
making the transition

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.071
from player to owner is something

00:02:04.071 --> 00:02:05.680
that's very exciting to me.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.540
I'm really excited to bring
my experience both in business

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.820
and in sports and in the locker room

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.240
and do really, really exciting things

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.610
and bring a world
championship to the city.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.720
In April, 2021, Alex Rodriguez

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.770
joined one of the most
exclusive clubs around;

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.250
folks who own a pro sports team.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.020
And while his name and his
fame made the headlines,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.860
it was his business empire

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.600
that allowed him to
actually close the deal.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.010
The easy to remember name, AROD Corp,

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.330
it's grown into a diversified
investor and operator,

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:40.790
taking a cue from mega firms
like Blackstone and KKR.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.210
AROD Corp has taken a
real estate portfolio

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.900
and expanded into venture
capital and private equity

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.900
and media, and like any
business in 2021, its own SPAC.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:52.630
I was more lucky than smart.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.750
I started investing very
small in real estate,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.750
simply as a hedge - as my
earning power would close,

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.070
my real estate would not only appreciate,

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.280
which would create more cash flow.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.590
And then I would take that cash flow

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.360
and I would buy more real estate

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.680
and other private equity type investments

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.140
where I generated more cash flow.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.910
So as my Yankee income went to zero,

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:16.980
hopefully my real estate,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.370
my businesses would grow over time.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.920
And while players nowadays are becoming

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.100
better and better business brands,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.910
few are able to achieve
the scale as AROD Corp has

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.170
in such a relatively short amount of time.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.030
But understanding how that
growth happened in business,

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.741
just like baseball, has to start at home.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.300
Part of what I love about business

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.980
is you get to build things
from nothing, right?

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.540
Like nothing, so I had two hip surgeries

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.980
and I never wanted to be the
old guy that couldn't get up,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.920
you know, the stairs,
so I built a flat house,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.830
all entertainment on that side.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.830
My room here, dad and
the girls on this side.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.971
But it had to be one story because A -

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.800
I'm always forgetting things.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.763
And B - I didn't wanna be like, oh my God,

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.993
I can't get up the steps
because of these hips.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.290
Yeah, I walk around here.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.570
I mean, first of all, the art's beautiful,

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:15.692
but tons of pictures of you and the girls.

00:04:15.692 --> 00:04:20.692
And I mean, you've
really made a family life

00:04:21.147 --> 00:04:23.040
for yourself here, tell me about that.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.600
Well, I'll tell you what I'm not at home.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.529
I'm not a cleanup hitter
and I'm not a CEO.

00:04:28.529 --> 00:04:31.340
The girls remind me every
day that I work for them.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.850
Yes.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.540
Daddy, I can see why you play baseball.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.062
People always ask me, what
do you do in your life?

00:04:37.062 --> 00:04:41.270
And I say the three BS: I do
baseball, now basketball too,

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.510
business and my babies.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.843
And that's kind of, that's my happy zone.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.560
It's funny through a pandemic,
we spent so much time here

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.690
and in my 46 years, the
toughest time in the world

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:54.870
that I've witnessed.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:55.810
On the other side of that,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.680
we were in here and we felt
safe and we felt loved.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.270
And it was like 90 days of
breakfast, lunch, and dinner

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.780
with my girls, it was a dream come true.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.773
And they got so sick of my dad
jokes, so sick of my dancing.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.330
Natasha, at least with
TikTok, I've been begging,

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.230
trying to bribe her to do one with me.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.190
She goes, daddy, you
have no swag, no drip.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.040
I'm not doing anything with you.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.110
A-Rod's dancing

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.090
may not get his daughter's approval,

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.000
but his following on social media is huge

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.640
with nearly 4 million
followers on Instagram alone.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.210
That number is larger than
his new basketball team,

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.300
the Yankees or the Red Sox,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.900
not to mention any other
single baseball player,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.440
current or former.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.880
Who has better taste in music?

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.763
Whose room is decorated nicer?

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.220
What is it that makes you
engage the way you do in social?

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.350
You know, Jason, a lot of
that came through my time

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.690
during my suspension.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.840
You know, I spent a whole
year serving a suspension.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.460
And in that time,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.591
I had an opportunity to
turn the lens inward,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.760
and understand why I was making
some of the good choices,

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.100
the poor choices, the bad choices,

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:11.420
and understand kind of conversation,
understand storytelling

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.640
and is something that I'm
enjoying; is still fairly new.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.340
We're not experts by any means,

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.900
but every time we look at
companies or opportunities

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.970
or partnerships, is data
driven and social driven,

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.970
and it's been quite a tool for us.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.400
That suspension, which obviously people

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.773
will forever associate you with.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.460
And there's an interesting
choice you make there

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.100
at that moment and then
in subsequent moments

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.900
to not lean out, to not retreat,

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.450
but to lean in to some extent,

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:47.470
and especially in the last couple years.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:48.697
Tell me about that.

00:06:48.697 --> 00:06:51.610
If you think about my career,
sure, I've hit home runs

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.700
and been part of a
world championship team,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.650
but I'm also,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.750
I'm fifth all time in the
history of strikeouts.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.540
I mean that means that only four people

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.593
in the history of mankind
have failed more than me.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.140
But I think as a result,

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.340
I've been, you know - PhD in failing,

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.360
but also an MBA of getting back up.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:12.820
Looking around at his house

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.920
and his selection of cars,

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.080
it's hard to say that A-Rod
has ever really failed,

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.250
but it's striking what he has built

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.263
considering where he came from.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.610
I was born in New York, raised in Miami.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.170
So when I got to Miami,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.270
I was around eight and
a half, nine years old.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.360
My father left when I was around 10,

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:39.970
and at home, it was mom, my brother, Joe,

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.400
and my sister Susie.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.530
They took the role of like,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.030
my brother became like
the secretary of sports,

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:49.070
my sister became the
secretary of education

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.900
and my mom just had to -
you know, make enough money

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.083
to pay our bills.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.630
I think, you know, my
father leaving us at 10

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:02.700
kinda left a huge vacant spot, right.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.700
And I think I needed to
then kind of fill that void

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.930
by having a collection of mentors.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.370
One of those earliest mentors

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.600
was his baseball coach, Rich Hofman,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.743
right here at Westminster High School.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.570
So you've walked through
this area a few times.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:21.403
Oh yeah.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:25.973
This brings back some
pretty cool memories.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:29.423
If you hit a home run here,

00:08:29.423 --> 00:08:31.900
it goes to the basketball
courts and the pool.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.078
And I kind of like it,

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:35.480
it's kind of like got this Fenway

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:37.990
kind of Wrigley field.
Yeah, exactly.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.470
Instead of the green monster,
you have the green pumps.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.290
Yes, exactly.

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:46.030
Well, it's really where I
really started to understand

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:48.860
the power of mentorship and dreaming big.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.100
And if you think about it,

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.530
I had one of the most
important conversations

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.020
with coach Rich Hofman.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:57.590
And that happened at 15, the
first day I walked on campus.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.540
He goes, "Look, Alex, you're
gonna come in this year.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.240
You're gonna have a good year.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.510
And then you're going
to the Junior Olympics

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.060
to represent the US.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.560
You'll do well there,
you'll get much stronger.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.570
By that time, you'll be like
the top junior in the country.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:13.130
And then your senior year,

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.520
you're gonna be the number
one pick in the country

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.487
and then you're gonna be
off to the big leagues."

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.350
Coach, you're talking to me?

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:26.030
He goes, "Yeah, I'm talking to you, Alex."

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.970
And Jason, I went home that
night and I believed him.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:33.640
It just like, it changed
my complete vision on me

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:34.980
and my future.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.323
And then I did everything in
my power not to let coach down.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.680
So as a leader today,
when I have young people,

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.680
it's my job to not only
give 'em the opportunity,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:50.410
but to push them and push them
to a level where they now see

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.940
the world in a different
way about themselves.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:54.780
That prediction came true.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.650
In the 1993 MLB draft, Rodriguez
was selected first overall

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.350
by the Seattle Mariners.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.040
And while his playing career
was just getting started,

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:04.350
by the age of 22,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.670
he'd invest in his first
piece of real estate.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.040
It was what I knew best.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.470
My mother, she wasn't a homeowner.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:13.470
We were renters, right?

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:14.990
We were tenants.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.470
And every 18 months we would move

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:19.930
because the landlord would
always raise the rents.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.300
And I remember as a young kid saying,

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:26.900
boy, if I can ever trade
places with the landlord

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:29.933
and they can be the tenant,
that would be amazing.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.530
I bought my first duplex

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.440
and then I owned that for a few years.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.290
I sold it for double.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:41.290
Bought fourplex, eightplex,
60 units, and off we went.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.500
When it came to those first investments,

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:46.090
you know, whenever he does something,

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:47.970
he's going in at a hundred percent.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.000
This is Jose Gomez;
everyone calls him Pepi.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.290
Along with Jose More,

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:55.420
he's the co-CEO of Newport
Property Construction,

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:58.060
and also a childhood friend of Rodriguez.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.410
From 2004 to 2007,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:06.350
we ended up acquiring 5,100
units between Florida and Texas.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:07.550
We renovated 'em.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.773
He would buy 'em, I would renovate them.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.010
And, you know, the rest was history.

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:14.370
Being a real estate investor,

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.880
especially in high growth
markets like Florida,

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.230
well, it hasn't been a straight line up

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:20.780
for the past two decades.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:22.820
Miami's high rise condos are monuments

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.500
to a burst property bubble.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.210
Almost half of Florida
mortgages are underwater.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.570
As we had this run up to 2007,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.390
prices were going up like this,

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.070
and then it just fell off a cliff.

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:35.140
The great recession crushed owners

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:36.820
and developers alike.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.370
Few were immune,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.780
except those that had
other sources of income

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.000
to take advantage of falling prices.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.570
Income like say one of the
richest baseball contracts

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:47.403
in history.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.380
Our huge advantage was
that Alex had W2 income

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.480
coming in from playing and
we had that stay in power.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.170
So we were able, even
when things started going,

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.040
you know, south for some people,

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.590
we were able to grow within
those difficult times.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:04.960
Monument Capital Management

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.090
has done more than 60 deals since 2012,

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:10.623
worth more than $900 million.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.020
The extent of that ambition
in real estate is evident here

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.503
at Grand Station in downtown Miami.

00:12:20.158 --> 00:12:21.670
The 30 story residential tower,

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.580
complete with a sprawling
amenity floor and pool

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:27.560
has sweeping views of downtown.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.380
It's a bet on Miami's growth

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.280
and it's a bet that's looking smarter

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.840
as Wall Street firms and other
companies look southward.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:38.600
People always question Miami,
"why do people live there?"

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.520
There's not any great jobs.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.800
You know, we don't have a lot
of financial services here.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.940
We don't have anybody
manufacturing anything,

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:48.590
but that's changing.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:52.910
People see this as a very
cool place to live and work.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.280
Now before was, oh, it was
always a cool place to live,

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.450
but where are you gonna work?

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.490
Now it's okay, the jobs are here first.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:12:59.716
Cool place to visit in the past.

00:12:59.716 --> 00:13:01.470
Right, yeah.
And now each pocket

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.390
is being built out.

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.200
I think Art Basel years
and years ago started that

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.160
and then there have
been a course of events

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.180
that have followed that
continue to enrich Miami.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:13.290
And I would say we're
still early as a city.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.650
But while Miami maybe home base,

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:18.280
Rodriguez's most aggressive
current real estate project

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.580
is buying, renovating, and
renting out single family homes

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.890
in markets across the country.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:25.690
And in this case, he's not just emulating

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:27.660
some of the world's biggest investors,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.530
but also getting into business with them,

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.270
including real estate
legend, Barry Sternlight.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:33.750
Just as behemoth like Blackstone

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:35.160
have amassed Single Family Homes.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.810
Rodriguez, through the
Tiber Capital Group,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.363
has snapped up 5,000 homes so far.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.130
We think we're still in the early earnings

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:44.390
of single family housing.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:46.880
Rents are going up about 18 to 19%.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:49.447
Once we find something, then...

00:13:49.447 --> 00:13:52.810
You know, this I learned
from Warren Buffet is,

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:55.360
you know, be disciplined but
when you find your pitch,

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:57.330
you know, don't go for
a single or a double,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:58.700
you know, go in and go in big

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:01.240
and that's exactly what
we've done multifamily,

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.750
and now with single family houses.

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.530
Going big has required Rodriguez

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:06.700
to build a team around him

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.190
and he is opted for a
delicate balance of homegrown

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.220
and outside hires.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.520
On the one side, friends
dating back to childhood

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:18.230
and adolescents, including
Erin, Pepi, and Jose.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:21.610
On the other newer additions,
such as Kelly Laferriere,

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:25.290
Stuart Zook, David Becker, and Lisa Peier.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.940
Managing the chemistry among
the small and growing team

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.440
is akin to the elusive
intangibles Rodriguez saw

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.480
in clubhouses when he was a player.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.610
Getting it right, well,
that's the difference

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:38.620
between a champion and an also-ran.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:39.630
Lisa, take it away.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:42.269
Sure, so I think next we're gonna have

00:14:42.269 --> 00:14:44.080
an update on Timberwolves from Kelly.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:44.913
Awesome.

00:14:44.913 --> 00:14:47.890
And VCP as it relates
to Timberwolves and Glen

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:51.390
Cool, so team's 11 and 11,

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:55.265
and one exciting development
is the 12/17 game

00:14:55.265 --> 00:14:57.223
against the Lakers is going to be on ESPN.

00:14:57.223 --> 00:15:00.160
So we were not on the ESPN
schedule, and now we are.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:01.810
At the AROD Corp offices,

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.130
you'll notice the memorabilia,

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:04.830
the pictures of famous athletes,

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:08.210
the realtime social data being
displayed on the TV wall,

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:10.300
but you'll also see pictures of Rodriguez

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:13.000
with some of the biggest
businessmen in the world.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.380
Alex has become a sponge.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.300
And he started that as an active player.

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:18.140
When he was on the road,

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:21.200
he would go and visit the
Jerry Reinsdorfs of the world

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:24.200
in his uniform and ask
them business questions.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.280
And he surrounded himself
with really good smart people.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.380
The wide network is
critical in sourcing deals,

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:32.350
including in the venture world
were where the AROD portfolio

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:34.610
has included more than 60 companies,

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.900
brands like Hims and
Hers, Gopuff and UFC gyms,

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.910
but having the best team doesn't
mean you win every at bat.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:43.910
Their SPAC, SLAM Corp,
was recently negotiating

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:46.190
a roughly $3 billion deal to merge

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:50.020
with collectible company
Panini that didn't materialize

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.810
after Panini stumbled.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.830
SLAM's still in search of a target,

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:58.070
but Rodriguez's belief in
taking swings, striking out,

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.260
and hitting big is also the
same way he would end up

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.343
with his own sports franchise.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:11.100
JLo and A-Rod are angling
to buy the New York Mets.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:12.573
They're not just angling,

00:16:12.573 --> 00:16:13.723
I mean, they are in the process

00:16:13.723 --> 00:16:15.860
of putting the money together.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.110
Rodriguez, along with a group

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:19.760
that included his
then-girlfriend, Jennifer Lopez,

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.850
entrepreneur Mark Lore and several others,

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:23.340
mounted a bid against front runner

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.080
and hedge fund billionaire,
Steven A. Cohen.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:27.970
These guys are all rich.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:29.340
You know, as far as we go,

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.040
they've made millions
and millions of dollars,

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:33.760
but we're talking about billions here.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.840
The very public process ultimately landed

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.700
A-Rod and JLo's consortium
as the runner up

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:42.800
with Cohen buying the
team for $2.4 billion.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.950
We are a little bit depressed about it.

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:49.050
We don't like to lose, and,
you know, we were really,

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.250
we started getting really excited

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.240
and thinking about what could be.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:54.760
And so when it just sort
of came to a complete halt,

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:57.070
there was sort of like
this shock to the system.

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.390
We were sort of depressed and down.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.810
Mark Lore is a New York born businessman

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:05.140
who most recently had been a
senior executive at Walmart.

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:07.730
He gotten there by selling
his company, jet.com

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:11.870
to the mega retailer and
stayed on to run that division.

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:14.770
And while A-Rod's partnership
with JLo would come undone,

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.340
he formed a close business
and personal connection

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:19.000
with Lore that's lasted.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:22.587
House rules, man, I told you no ties, man.

00:17:22.587 --> 00:17:24.800
That's my favorite tie!

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:29.280
You grew up in a sort of very similar way,

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.460
and from very humble beginnings.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:32.980
I love Mark, I love him like a brother,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:35.270
and he's a great partner
and a great friend.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.730
And, you know, he sleeps at
my house, I sleep at his house

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:40.800
and we both have this
drive and this mission

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:42.200
that I want him to win in the worst way

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:45.180
and I think he does the same for me.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:49.350
I wanna show you this clip
because it spoke to me a lot

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.300
about the depth of your relationship.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:55.490
Yeah, I think this is getting
a little bit deeper now,

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:59.083
but I think it comes down
to our dads, you know.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.580
We both felt like, you know,
we had something to prove

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:07.260
in order to sort of feel loved really.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.270
And when you did something spectacular,

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:14.163
you felt, oh, maybe, you know.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:18.720
And I think he similarly had felt that too

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:22.213
where if you wanted to feel
loved, you had to do big things.

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:25.930
What do you make of that?

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.220
I never thought about it that way,

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:32.080
but I know that when my father left at 10,

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:35.830
it left a huge hole in
my heart, in my stomach,

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:40.780
and I knew that I had to do better.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:43.380
And I think before therapy,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.630
it was probably better
for the wrong things.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:50.233
I think post and current, I
look at it as just my purpose.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:55.970
But I also know that I'm enough now.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.663
And probably before
that, I thought I wasn't.

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:00.860
The deep connection with Lore

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:05.040
led to a new $400 million
venture fund called VCP,

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:07.210
and notably allowed them to quickly pivot

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:09.770
to a successful deal in sports ownership;

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:11.820
their pursuit of the
Minnesota Timberwolves

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.310
and Minnesota Lynx.

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:14.247
He called me and he said,

00:19:14.247 --> 00:19:15.990
"Hey, are you serious about this?"

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.760
He said, "I won't do it unless
you're serious about it."

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:20.710
And I said, I'm serious about it.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:22.540
Are you serious about it?

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:25.620
And again, a minute
conversation, we're both in,

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.352
and we've probably got the
deal done in six or seven days.

00:19:29.352 --> 00:19:31.440
NBA ownership has radically transformed

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:32.760
in the past decade.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:35.480
Its tech executives like
Steve Ballmer and Joe Tsai

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:38.330
and Wall Street investors
like Mark Lasry, Josh Harris,

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.740
and Tony Ressler have snapped up teams.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:44.340
There's a supply demand
imbalance where anyone,

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.540
you know, most people, if they could,

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.210
would own a professional sports
team and there's, you know,

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.160
very high barriers to entry

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.373
and, you know, very limited supply.

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:54.950
In the case of the Timberwolves,

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:58.713
Glen Taylor bought the team
in 1994 for $88 million.

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:02.580
The sale to Rodriguez's
group values the franchise,

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.610
paired with the four
time WNBA champion, Lynx,

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:07.893
at $1.5 billion.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.030
And that's relatively affordable.

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:15.500
A-Rod's old team, the Yankees,
is worth more than $5 billion

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.110
according to several estimates.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:21.130
And while ownership is changing,
so too are the players.

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:24.273
I think players today
want more in every sport.

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:27.100
They're into fashion.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:29.380
They wanna be good investors.

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:31.180
They wanna learn about venture.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:34.140
The NBA players today
that I've been around,

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.670
some of them are more prepped at 20

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:40.170
than some of the guys that
I played with at were 50

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:43.340
and meeting with our athletes
and talking about business

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:47.840
and them reaching out to me,
it's great, it's humbling,

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:49.980
and it's really impressive.

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:50.813
And if anything,

00:20:50.813 --> 00:20:53.900
their ownership has maybe
gotten too close to the players

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:55.990
with the team being fined $250,000

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:59.200
for violating league rules
with an off season workout

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:00.853
in A-Rod's hometown of Miami.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:04.870
But buying a basketball
team instead of the Mets

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:08.300
might accomplish a goal he
thought was unreachable,

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.860
actually making him cool to his daughters.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:15.420
I recently went to a Sixers
game and my little one,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.633
Ella, who's 13, she goes,

00:21:17.633 --> 00:21:20.580
"Dad, I really like this basketball thing.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:25.440
Like one day maybe I get to
run the business here one day."

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:26.400
Really?

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:27.650
And I was the like,

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.670
oh my God, that's like the
greatest thing I've ever heard.

00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:33.450
And she goes, "You know, it's like,

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.490
you know, baseball's a little
slower, but this stuff,

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:37.040
I can really get into it."

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:38.130
I said, whoa, baseball's great too.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.520
It's just, you know, it's
more of a thinking man's game.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:42.380
It's like chess, not checkers.

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:43.440
And she goes, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, whatever.

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:45.417
I don't like chess either but," she says,

00:21:45.417 --> 00:21:47.960
"This basketball thing,
I have like real passion,

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:50.550
is like the entertainment
and I have thoughts

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:52.480
and I have ideas and I,"

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:54.450
and she thinks much more like a producer.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:55.860
She likes dancing.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:58.290
And I mean, to me, that's just like,

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.400
boy, that's the great thing
about sports and music.

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:02.130
It brings people together.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:04.330
And it certainly did
that for us that night.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:08.780
You have built this amazing business.

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.020
You know, there are people
who are watching this

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:11.760
who's like this guy!

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:15.070
You know, it's like
they'll always remember you

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:17.500
for your baseball career
and a baseball career

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:20.290
that was this controversial,
to say the least.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.130
You could be living on
an island somewhere,

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:24.170
you don't have to do this.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:27.210
I thought about tapping
out a bunch of times.

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:29.180
I would wake up in the middle of the night

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.280
and being tears going,
like how in the world

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.230
do I get Pocket Aces and figure
out a way to screw this up?

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:39.080
That was a very painful time for me.

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:43.030
And I think the year suspension allowed me

00:22:43.030 --> 00:22:46.270
to not only put lipstick
on the house and paint it

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:47.750
and fix the windows,

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.630
it allowed me to tear
the entire house down

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:53.253
and start building it one brick at a time.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.563
And in that process, I became
even closer to my daughters.

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:01.590
And when I think about those mistakes

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:04.540
and the lessons learned, I think about,

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:06.340
okay, but you know what?

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:07.900
That was baseball.

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:11.933
And I had good moments and
I had very tough moments,

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:16.890
but clean slate, when it
comes to being a father,

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:19.290
to being a partner, to
being a businessman,

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:20.720
to being a friend.

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:25.280
So I draw some incredible
motivation through,

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:29.423
man, something that sucked,
something that I did to myself.

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:35.410
I say to myself, in the darkest hour,

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:37.563
boy, if I ever get another opportunity,

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:40.160
I'm not gonna screw that up again.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:43.430
And I'm trying my best not to do that.

